1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,686
This is the revision for module four, Feature Engineering, Transformation and Analytical Structure.

2
00:00:07,686 --> 00:00:08,928
The takeaway is:

3
00:00:08,928 --> 00:00:12,655
transform data and extract meaningful structure.

4
00:00:12,655 --> 00:00:19,176
We recap lectures twenty three to thirty and finish with questions to test yourself.

5
00:00:19,976 --> 00:00:23,173
Lectures twenty three and twenty four.

6
00:00:23,173 --> 00:00:27,380
Transforming changes shape; scaling changes units.

7
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:39,244
Log is a fixed-strength transform that can over-correct; Box-Cox lets the data choose the power, and Yeo-Johnson handles zeros and negatives.

8
00:00:39,244 --> 00:00:45,134
Standard, min-max and robust scalers are linear and never change skew.

9
00:00:45,134 --> 00:00:50,772
Min-max is fragile with outliers; robust scaling is best with them.

10
00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:55,736
Transform first, then scale, and fit on training data only.

11
00:00:56,536 --> 00:00:59,583
Lectures twenty five and twenty six.

12
00:00:59,583 --> 00:01:06,777
Construct features with totals, ratios, flags, logs and bands, never from the target.

13
00:01:06,777 --> 00:01:11,263
Interactions state that the effect of A depends on B.

14
00:01:11,263 --> 00:01:15,410
Polynomial features grow fast, so use low degree.

15
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:19,726
Too many columns cause the curse of dimensionality.

16
00:01:19,726 --> 00:01:26,920
Select features with filters, wrappers or embedded methods, using training rows only.

17
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:31,116
Lectures twenty seven and twenty eight.

18
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:33,554
V I F finds linked features.

19
00:01:33,554 --> 00:01:45,309
P C A finds new uncorrelated axes; scale first, read variance explained with scree and Kaiser rules, and name components from loadings.

20
00:01:45,309 --> 00:01:52,363
L D A uses labels to separate classes, with at most classes minus one components.

21
00:01:52,363 --> 00:01:58,632
t-S N E and U M A P are for pictures only and should never feed a model.

22
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:02,252
Lectures twenty nine and thirty.

23
00:02:02,252 --> 00:02:06,658
Refine hypotheses and set the decision rule first.

24
00:02:06,658 --> 00:02:11,945
Split first, cross-validate on training rows, and test once.

25
00:02:11,945 --> 00:02:14,589
Data leakage makes scores lie:

26
00:02:14,589 --> 00:02:21,110
target leakage, contamination, preprocessing leakage and sampling leakage.

27
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:29,129
For skewed targets judge by recall, F one and R O C A U C, and resample training rows only.

28
00:02:29,129 --> 00:02:34,064
An insight is observation, evidence, meaning and action.

29
00:02:34,864 --> 00:02:36,800
The labs for this module:

30
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:42,916
practicals twenty three and twenty four compare log, Box-Cox and three scalers.

31
00:02:42,916 --> 00:02:48,181
Practicals twenty five and twenty six construct and select features.

32
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:52,285
Practicals twenty seven and twenty eight apply P C A.

33
00:02:52,285 --> 00:02:59,176
Practicals twenty nine and thirty build the full pipeline and an industry-style notebook.

34
00:02:59,976 --> 00:03:02,244
Test yourself, part one.

35
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:03,473
Question one:

36
00:03:03,473 --> 00:03:08,765
a symmetric column becomes skewed after a log transform.

37
00:03:08,765 --> 00:03:11,033
Why, and what do you do?

38
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:11,695
Answer:

39
00:03:11,695 --> 00:03:21,240
log is too strong for it, so leave symmetric features alone or use Box-Cox, which picks a weak power.

40
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:22,469
Question two:

41
00:03:22,469 --> 00:03:27,950
min-max scaling squeezes most values below zero point one.

42
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:28,328
Why?

43
00:03:28,328 --> 00:03:28,990
Answer:

44
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:36,456
one extreme value sets the maximum; use a transform first or the robust scaler.

45
00:03:37,256 --> 00:03:39,378
Test yourself, part two.

46
00:03:39,378 --> 00:03:40,704
Question three:

47
00:03:40,704 --> 00:03:45,124
why must feature selection use only training rows?

48
00:03:45,124 --> 00:03:45,742
Answer:

49
00:03:45,742 --> 00:03:53,433
if test rows help choose features, the test score is no longer honest; that is leakage.

50
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:54,671
Question four:

51
00:03:54,671 --> 00:04:01,212
a model has recall zero point nine nine on a test set after over-sampling.

52
00:04:01,212 --> 00:04:03,157
What should worry you?

53
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:03,776
Answer:

54
00:04:03,776 --> 00:04:10,052
if you over-sampled before splitting, copies of rows sit in both parts.

55
00:04:10,052 --> 00:04:11,290
Question five:

56
00:04:11,290 --> 00:04:14,737
why is t-S N E not used as model input?

57
00:04:14,737 --> 00:04:15,356
Answer:

58
00:04:15,356 --> 00:04:23,312
its axes are not stable or meaningful, it changes with seed, and it cannot place new rows.

59
00:04:24,112 --> 00:04:27,703
That is module four, and the whole course.

60
00:04:27,703 --> 00:04:38,648
Transform then scale; engineer then select; reduce dimensions with P C A; split first and test once; and report honest insights.

61
00:04:38,648 --> 00:04:47,626
In the next videos we cover the lab assessment, the final project, and the complete workflow in one page.

62
00:04:47,626 --> 00:04:49,336
Thanks for watching.

